  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They led from the first inning, then gave it back in one brutal stretch.",
    "what_this_means": "The Athletics had the lead. That is the story of this game. They scored two in the first, held it through four innings, then the bullpen unraveled in the seventh and eighth. Jack Perkins allowed three runs in 1.1 innings and the night was finished. The offense did convert two runners in scoring position, which matters, but it was not enough to survive the late collapse. They left nine on base total, which includes three critical missed chances in the second and third innings when the margin was still tight. The game tied at six through the sixth, and that was the last moment it felt winnable. Jeffrey Springs gave them a clean line through 6.1 innings. Three hits, four runs. But the bullpen's failure to hold made his work meaningless.",
    "what_to_watch": "The fragile win streak is over and the fragile pattern shows itself again. Early lead, controlled middle innings, late-inning breakdown. Springs was good enough. The lineup found some runners. But nine RISP situations produced only two runs, and when Perkins walked out in the seventh, nobody was waiting with a lead. This is what happens when a team leaves that much on base and leans on relief that is not ready. The division is tight, the margin is small, and these are the games that are supposed to hold.",
